      "60 g of protein in about 650 calories",
      "Much less sugar per serving than the big gainers",
      "28 servings per bag"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About half the calories, so true hard gainers may need two servings",
      "About $7.69 per 1,000 calories, double Super Mass Gainer's calorie cost",
      "High upfront price for a 10 lb bag"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The better buy if protein is your goal and calories are a bonus rather than the point.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "60 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 650",
      "Third-party testing": "Banned Substance Tested mark (label)",
      "Cost per 25 g": "About $2.08 (Amazon price seen)"
    },
    "amazonUrl": "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B002DYIZM6"
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Dymatize Super Mass Gainer Review",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/dymatize-super-mass-gainer-review/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Dymatize Super Mass Gainer third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Dymatize says Super Mass Gainer is Banned Substance Tested, but its product pages do not name the testing program, and we could not find a public registry listing for this product. That is fine reassurance for most recreational lifters. Drug-tested athletes should ask Dymatize which program certifies it and look the product up in that program's database first."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Dymatize Super Mass Gainer healthy or clean?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is useful for people who need extra calories but not especially clean. Maltodextrin is the first ingredient, a serving has 245 g of carbohydrate and 14 g of added sugar, and the formula contains sucralose, acesulfame potassium, artificial flavors and carrageenan. For anyone who already eats enough, it mostly adds fat. Naked Mass is the cleaner-label alternative."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Dymatize Super Mass Gainer contain creatine?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, a small amount. Creatine monohydrate appears in the less-than-2% part of the ingredient list, and the Amazon listing gives it as 1 g per serving. That is well below the 3 to 5 g a day used in most creatine research, so add a separate creatine monohydrate if you want the full effect. Serious Mass lists 5 g."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Super Mass Gainer have artificial sweeteners or lactose?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes to both. The label lists sucralose and acesulfame potassium plus natural and artificial flavors, and the protein blend includes whey protein concentrate and milk protein isolate, which contain some lactose. It declares milk and soy as allergens. People who are lactose intolerant may get bloating or gas, especially from a full serving."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which Dymatize Super Mass Gainer flavor is best?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buyers most often praise Rich Chocolate and Gourmet Vanilla, describing both as rich and milkshake-like, especially in milk. Fruity Pebbles divides opinion more, with some reviewers finding it very sweet at this serving size. Chocolate also lists 3 g of fiber and slightly less added sugar than Vanilla, per Dymatize's labels at the time of writing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the most common complaints about Super Mass Gainer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buyers most often mention bloating, gas and a heavy full feeling after a full serving, along with the large volume of powder per shake and clumping when it is not blended. Some note the flavors are very sweet. Most of these ease with half servings, a blender and liquid added first. Price is rarely a complaint, as it is cheap for a gainer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many calories are in a scoop of Super Mass Gainer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Dymatize measures the serving in cups, not scoops: 2 1/2 cups (333 g) gives 1,280 calories and 52 g of protein, per the Gourmet Vanilla label. So one cup of powder is roughly 510 calories and 21 g of protein, and a half serving is about 640 calories. Mixing with whole milk instead of water adds roughly 150 calories per 8 oz."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "dymatize super mass gainer protein powder",
      "super mass gainer protein powder",
      "dymatize super mass gainer review",
      "dymatize mass gainer",
      "super mass gainer calories"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Weight Gain Picks",
    link: "/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Dymatize Super Mass Gainer Review</Fragment>
      For hard gainers deciding whether Dymatize's 1,280-calorie shake is worth buying: the label, the testing claim, cost per 25 g and how it compares with Serious Mass.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#whats-in-it">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#calories-serving">Calories and Serving Size</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyer-feedback">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs-serious-mass">Super Mass vs Serious Mass</a>
      <a href="#is-it-healthy">Is It Healthy?</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Dymatize Super Mass Gainer is the cheapest big gainer we priced, giving 1,280 calories and 52 g of protein per serving for about $2.10 to $2.40 per 25 g of protein. Whether it suits you depends on how many calories you are actually missing. Below we read the label, check the testing claim, run the cost and compare it with Serious Mass and Naked Mass.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Dymatize Super Mass Gainer: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dymatize Super Mass Gainer is a cheap, effective way to drink about 1,280 calories and 52 g of protein in one shake, and it is the lowest cost per calorie among the big gainers we priced. Its weak points are a carb-heavy, maltodextrin-first formula, two artificial sweeteners and a Banned Substance Tested claim that does not name the testing program.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you are underweight or a genuine hard gainer, you struggle to eat enough food, and you want the most calories per dollar. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you already eat enough and just want more protein, you need a named sport certification, you avoid sucralose or carrageenan, or you have a medical reason to limit sugar and carbohydrate.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Per Serving, Gourmet Vanilla (label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1,280</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">52 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">245 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Carbohydrate</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2.10-$2.40</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein (Amazon)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Super Mass Gainer Sizes and the Gainers to Compare
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on 20 November 2026; label figures are per the brand at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="whats-in-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in Dymatize Super Mass Gainer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Super Mass Gainer is mostly maltodextrin plus a five-part milk protein blend. Per <a href="https://dymatize.com/products/super-mass-gainer-gourmet-vanilla" rel="nofollow">Dymatize's Gourmet Vanilla page</a> at the time of writing, one serving is 2 1/2 cups of powder (333 g) mixed with 24 oz of water, and it gives 1,280 calories, 52 g of protein, 245 g of total carbohydrate, 23 g of total sugars including 14 g of added sugars, 10 g of fat, 2.5 g of saturated fat, 135 mg of cholesterol, 450 mg of sodium and 0 g of fiber.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ingredient list starts with maltodextrin, which is why carbohydrate supplies about 77% of the calories. The protein blend is whey protein concentrate, milk protein isolate, whey protein isolate, whey protein hydrolysate and micellar casein, which is where Dymatize's &quot;fast and slow absorbing&quot; line comes from. A sunflower creamer made from sunflower oil, maltodextrin and sodium caseinate adds most of the fat and some of the creamy texture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Below 2% of the formula sit creatine monohydrate, fructose, natural and artificial flavors, a vitamin and mineral blend, a gum blend of cellulose gum, xanthan gum and carrageenan, potassium chloride, soy lecithin, sunflower lecithin, acesulfame potassium and sucralose. The label declares milk and soy. The Amazon listing gives the creatine dose as 1 g per serving, which is a fifth of the usual 5 g daily dose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dymatize lists 10.7 g of BCAAs and 5.1 g of leucine per serving. The vitamin blend covers 14 vitamins and minerals, per Dymatize, including 950 mg of calcium (73% of the Daily Value), 150 mcg of iodine (100%) and 8 mg of pantothenic acid (160%). The <a href="https://dymatize.com/products/super-mass-gainer-rich-chocolate" rel="nofollow">Rich Chocolate page</a> shows a slightly different panel: a 335 g serving with the same 1,280 calories and 52 g of protein, 11 g of fat, 21 g of total sugars (12 g added), 3 g of fiber from cocoa and 580 mg of sodium.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per serving (label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Gourmet Vanilla</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rich Chocolate</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Serving size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">333 g (2 1/2 cups)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">335 g (2 1/2 cups)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,280</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,280</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">52 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">52 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbohydrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">245 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">245 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Total / added sugars</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g / 14 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g / 12 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">11 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">450 mg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">580 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fiber</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 g</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Fruity Pebbles, the third flavor, matches Gourmet Vanilla on Dymatize's own page, but its Amazon title lists 1,290 calories and 248 g of carbohydrate, and Dymatize's overview page says calories run up to 1,310 and carbohydrate up to 252 g depending on flavor. Read the panel on the bag you buy, because the flavors are close but not identical.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Dymatize Super Mass Gainer Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dymatize says Super Mass Gainer is Banned Substance Tested, but its product pages do not name the program that does the testing, and we could not confirm a listing for this product in a public certification registry. The claim appears as a benefit icon on the <a href="https://dymatize.com/super-mass-gainer" rel="nofollow">Super Mass Gainer product page</a> and on each flavor page, without a certificate number or a link to a database entry.
          </p>

          <p>
            That distinction matters for anyone tested for drugs in sport. Programs such as Informed Choice, Informed Sport and NSF Certified for Sport publish searchable databases where you can check an exact product and often an exact batch. Dymatize's ISO100 and Elite powders have carried the Informed Choice logo on retailer listings, which our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize protein powder review</a> covers, but we found no equivalent public listing for Super Mass Gainer when we checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a recreational lifter trying to gain weight, a banned substance claim from a large brand owned by BellRing Brands is reasonable reassurance. For a college, Olympic or professional athlete, it is not enough. Ask Dymatize which program tests the gainer, then search that program's database for the product name before you use it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Banned substance testing also says nothing about heavy metals or label accuracy. We found no published heavy metal result for Super Mass Gainer. If contaminant testing is your priority, Naked Mass carries an NSF Contents Certified claim per Naked, and our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF-certified protein powder guide</a> lists products with registry-verified certification.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A logo is not a listing</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you are drug-tested, use only a product you can find by name in the certifier's own database. A Banned Substance Tested icon without a named program cannot be checked.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="calories-serving" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Many Calories Are in Super Mass Gainer, and How Much Should You Take?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A full serving of Super Mass Gainer gives 1,280 calories, which is more than half of the 2,000-calorie reference diet on the label. That serving is 2 1/2 cups of powder, 333 g, mixed with 24 oz of water. Mixed with whole milk instead, the shake rises to roughly 1,700 calories, since 24 oz of whole milk adds about 450 calories and 24 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most people do not need the full serving, and many buyers report splitting it. A half serving, about 1 1/4 cups or 167 g, gives roughly 640 calories and 26 g of protein, close to the calories of a large meal. That also makes a 6 lb bag last 16 shakes instead of 8, which halves the cost per shake to about $2.50.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical way to dose it is to work out your gap. Track what you eat for three or four days, compare it with what you need to gain about 0.25 to 0.5 kg a week, and use the gainer to fill the difference. If you are 500 calories short, a half serving covers it. If you are 1,200 calories short, a full serving does, but a gap that size usually means meals need fixing as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing matters less than the daily total. Dymatize suggests the shake after training, but a gainer works the same as food: it adds to the day's calories whenever you drink it. Splitting a full serving into two half shakes, one after training and one between meals, is easier on the stomach than 1,280 calories at once and makes it less likely you will skip a meal because you feel full.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How fast will it add weight?</h3>

          <p>
            A common rule of thumb is that a surplus of about 3,500 calories adds roughly a pound of body weight, though the real figure varies by person and by how much of the gain is muscle. On that rough basis, a daily half serving on top of a maintenance diet would add about a pound every week to ten days. Pair the surplus with resistance training and enough protein, around 1.6 g per kg of body weight a day per our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a>, so more of that gain is muscle.
          </p>
        </div>

