              <summary><h3>Does Super Mass Gainer have artificial sweeteners or lactose?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes to both. The label lists sucralose and acesulfame potassium plus natural and artificial flavors, and the protein blend includes whey protein concentrate and milk protein isolate, which contain some lactose. It declares milk and soy as allergens. People who are lactose intolerant may get bloating or gas, especially from a full serving.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Dymatize Super Mass Gainer flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buyers most often praise Rich Chocolate and Gourmet Vanilla, describing both as rich and milkshake-like, especially in milk. Fruity Pebbles divides opinion more, with some reviewers finding it very sweet at this serving size. Chocolate also lists 3 g of fiber and slightly less added sugar than Vanilla, per Dymatize's labels at the time of writing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the most common complaints about Super Mass Gainer?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buyers most often mention bloating, gas and a heavy full feeling after a full serving, along with the large volume of powder per shake and clumping when it is not blended. Some note the flavors are very sweet. Most of these ease with half servings, a blender and liquid added first. Price is rarely a complaint, as it is cheap for a gainer.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How many calories are in a scoop of Super Mass Gainer?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Dymatize measures the serving in cups, not scoops: 2 1/2 cups (333 g) gives 1,280 calories and 52 g of protein, per the Gourmet Vanilla label. So one cup of powder is roughly 510 calories and 21 g of protein, and a half serving is about 640 calories. Mixing with whole milk instead of water adds roughly 150 calories per 8 oz.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

